      "id": 1,
      "text": "Norcross was named by merging Henry Norton’s and Judson Cross’s surnames after they donated land when the Great Northern Railway chose their ranch for a depot.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lakesnwoods.com/Norcross.htm",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s first school, called New School, opened in 1903 and later served as the Norwegian Lutheran Church when a newer school was built.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norcross%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A great fire in 1928 razed every building on Norcross’s south side, reshaping the town’s downtown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norcross%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Norcross Town Hall, built in the late 1920s, became the town’s social hub for dances, skating, and community events.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norcross%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Norcross once hosted three churches; Our Savior’s Lutheran Church, now a private home, celebrated its 90th anniversary in 1991.",
